<div class="art-head"><div><div class="up" style="color:#8A8A8A">Beauty · Sun protection · K-beauty · Updated October 2026</div><h1 class="hd">Beauty of Joseon Relief Sun Review: Rice + Probiotics vs Aqua-Fresh, Ingredients and White Cast</h1><p class="serif stand">One of the most searched sunscreens in the US, and one I like. Here is what is actually in it, which UV filters it uses, what the lab results say, and which version I would pick for which skin.</p><div class="hand" style="transform:rotate(-2deg);font-size:34px;margin-top:10px">dewy, creamy, no white cast</div></div>
<div class="ph art-hero"><span>Image 1 · Hero<br><em>Beauty of Joseon Relief Sun tube on my bathroom shelf</em></span></div></div></section>
<section class="p art-grid"><div class="art-body">

<p>You cannot scroll K-beauty for five minutes without seeing this sunscreen, and I understand the hype: it is cheap, it feels creamy like a moisturizer and it leaves no white cast. I like it, and I like its lighter sibling, the Aqua-Fresh “Aqua Rice” version, too. But the reviews are not unanimous, so here is the full picture.</p>
<p>There are two versions: the original <strong>Relief Sun: Rice + Probiotics</strong> and <strong>Relief Sun Aqua-Fresh: Rice + B5</strong>. I went through the brand’s official pages and public reviews. I’m not a dermatologist, and this is not medical advice.</p>

<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>My take after using it since February 2025:</strong> I prefer Aqua Rice because it is lighter, while the original is richer and perfect for dry skin. Neither stings my eyes.</li>
<li>It is a <strong>chemical sunscreen</strong> with SPF 50+ and PA++++, so it has no zinc or titanium white cast.</li>
<li>The formula leads with <strong>30% rice extract</strong> and fermented grain extracts, and the texture is creamy and moisturizing.</li>
<li>The brand shows lab results of <strong>SPF 52.5 and SPF 63.1</strong> from two labs, without sharing the number of test subjects.</li>
<li>The original suits normal, dry and combination skin. For oily skin the brand points to <strong>Aqua-Fresh</strong>.</li>
<li>Reviewers split on two things: it can feel too dewy on very oily skin, and it can sting the eyes for some people.</li>
</ul>

<h2>My experience: using it since February 2025</h2>
<p>I have normal skin, and Beauty of Joseon has been one of my sunscreens since February 2025. I like the texture, the ingredients, the protection and how easy it is to apply. Neither version stings my eyes, and my skin looks a little shiny with them on.</p>
<p>I prefer the <strong>Aqua Rice</strong> version because it is lighter. Its texture is closer to a milk lotion than a cream. The <strong>original</strong> is greasier and you feel more of it on the skin, which is exactly why it is perfect for dry skin. If you know my <a href="/beauty/round-lab-birch-juice-sunscreen-review/">Round Lab Birch Juice review</a>, here is the easiest comparison: the original feels similar to the Birch Juice sunscreen, and Aqua Rice feels similar to the Birch Juice sun serum. I would recommend it to everyone, and I would buy it again.</p>

<div class="hand" style="transform:rotate(-2deg);font-size:34px;margin:6px 0 20px;color:#C2517A">aqua rice is my pick</div>

<h2>Beauty of Joseon Relief Sun at a glance</h2>
<ProductFacts title="Relief Sun: Rice + Probiotics, at a glance" rows={[['Good for','Daily sun protection that feels like a moisturizer, under makeup or alone'],['Hero ingredient','30% rice extract with fermented grain extracts'],['Protection','SPF 50+ PA++++ with four chemical UV filters'],['Size and price','50 ml. $18.00 on the brand’s US store (checked October 2026)'],['How to use','Two full fingers on face and neck as the last morning step; reapply every 2 to 3 hours'],['FYI','Chemical filters can sting the eyes; very oily skin may prefer Aqua-Fresh']]}/>
<p style="color:#8A8A8A;font-size:14px">Source: <a href="https://beautyofjoseon.com/products/relief-sun-rice-probiotics" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Beauty of Joseon official product page</a>. Prices change, so check the page before you buy.</p>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 2<br><em>The Relief Sun tube next to the rest of my sunscreens</em></span></div>

<h2>What’s inside: the full ingredient list</h2>
<p>I took the list from the brand’s official page and not from a retailer. In order, it reads:</p>
<details class="inci" style="margin:0 0 22px"><summary class="up" style="cursor:pointer;color:#8A8A8A;padding:10px 0">Show the full ingredient list</summary><p><em>Water, Oryza Sativa (Rice) Extract (30%), Dibutyl Adipate, Propanediol, Diethylamino Hydroxybenzoyl Hexyl Benzoate, Polymethylsilsesquioxane, Ethylhexyl Triazone, Niacinamide, Methylene Bis-benzotriazolyl Tetramethylbutylphenol, Coco-caprylate/Caprate, Caprylyl Methicone, Diethylhexyl Butamido Triazone, Glycerin, Butylene Glycol, Oryza Sativa (Rice) Germ Extract, Camellia Sinensis Leaf Extract, Lactobacillus/Pumpkin Ferment Extract, Bacillus/Soybean Ferment Extract, Saccharum Officinarum (Sugarcane) Extract, Macrocystis Pyrifera (Kelp) Extract, Cocos Nucifera (Coconut) Fruit Extract, Panax Ginseng Root Extract, Camellia Sinensis Leaf Extract, Monascus/Rice Ferment, Pentylene Glycol, Behenyl Alcohol, Poly C10-30 Alkyl Acrylate, Polyglyceryl-3 Methylglucose Distearate, Decyl Glucoside, Tromethamine, Carbomer, Acrylates/C10-30 Alkyl Acrylate Crosspolymer, 1,2-Hexanediol, Sodium Stearoyl Glutamate, Polyacrylate Crosspolymer-6, Ethylhexylglycerin, Adenosine, Xanthan Gum, Tocopherol, Lactobacillus/Rice Ferment, Aspergillus Ferment, Saccharomyces/Rice Ferment Filtrate.</em></p></details>
<ProductInside intro="The brand builds the formula around rice extract and fermented grains, with four chemical UV filters doing the sun protection." items={[['Rice extract (30%)','The second ingredient. The brand describes it as rich in vitamins B, C and E, amino acids and minerals, for moisture and antioxidant support.'],['Fermented grain extracts','Pumpkin, soybean and rice ferments that the brand says soothe, calm and hydrate.'],['Four UV filters','Ethylhexyl triazone, diethylamino hydroxybenzoyl hexyl benzoate, diethylhexyl butamido triazone and methylene bis-benzotriazolyl tetramethylbutylphenol give the SPF 50+ PA++++.']]} also="niacinamide, glycerin, adenosine, tocopherol, green tea, ginseng, kelp and coconut extracts"/>
<h3>Worth checking</h3>
<p>The formula contains plant and ferment extracts, so patch-test if your skin is reactive. Chemical filters can sting if they get into the eyes, so keep it a little away from the lash line.</p>

<h2>Which skin concerns it suits</h2>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Concern</th><th>What to expect</th></tr>
<tr><td>Daily sun protection</td><td>SPF 50+ PA++++ with lab results above 50 in both labs the brand shows.</td></tr>
<tr><td>White cast</td><td>The brand says none, and it uses chemical filters.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dry or dehydrated skin</td><td>Creamy and moisturizing; the brand says you can layer it over a moisturizer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oily or acne-prone skin</td><td>Some find the original too dewy. The brand points oily skin to Aqua-Fresh.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sensitive skin</td><td>Mixed. The brand reports a soothing test for Aqua-Fresh, while some reviewers report stinging or breakouts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Under makeup</td><td>Reviewers describe it as layering well, though the dewy finish may need a setting step for some.</td></tr></table>
<p style="color:#8A8A8A;font-size:14px">This table summarizes the brand’s claims and public reviews, not a clinical test.</p>

<h2>Chemical or mineral, and why white cast matters</h2>
<p>Sunscreens work with either mineral filters (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide) or chemical filters. Mineral ones sit on the skin and can leave a pale layer, especially on deeper skin tones. Chemical ones absorb into a thin, clear film, so there is usually no white cast. That is the main reason Relief Sun is popular, and it is also why some people notice more stinging around the eyes. The FDA says what matters most is choosing a broad-spectrum sunscreen with SPF 15 or higher, using enough, and reapplying about every two hours.</p>

<h2>Does it work? SPF, PA and the brand’s lab results</h2>
<p>The brand’s page shows two lab tests for the original. Lab 1 in Korea found <strong>SPF 52.5 ± 5.8</strong> and a UVA protection factor of 16.1 (PA++++). Lab 2 in Spain found <strong>SPF 63.1 ± 0.6</strong> and a UVA protection factor of 19 (PA++++). Both are above the labelled SPF 50. The page does not say how many people were tested, so I treat the numbers as supportive, not as an independent verdict. In real life, results depend mostly on how much you apply: the brand says two full fingers on the face and neck.</p>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 3<br><em>Two fingers of Relief Sun, showing the creamy texture</em></span></div>

<h2>Rice + Probiotics vs Aqua-Fresh</h2>
<p>If you are choosing between the two, this is how they differ based on the brand’s pages. The Aqua-Fresh details come from the brand’s EU/UK page, and filters may differ by market, so check the label of the one you buy.</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th></th><th>Rice + Probiotics (original)</th><th>Aqua-Fresh: Rice + B5</th></tr>
<tr><td>Texture</td><td>Creamy, moisturizing</td><td>Light fluid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Key ingredients</td><td>30% rice extract, fermented grains</td><td>Rice seed water, panthenol (B5)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protection</td><td>SPF 50+ PA++++</td><td>SPF 50+ PA++++</td></tr>
<tr><td>UV filters</td><td>Four (listed above)</td><td>Five, including Mexoryl SX and Tinosorb S (EU/UK page)</td></tr>
<tr><td>On my skin (normal)</td><td>Greasier, you feel it more. Perfect for dry skin</td><td>Lighter, closer to a milk lotion than a cream</td></tr>
<tr><td>Feels most like</td><td>Round Lab Birch Juice sunscreen</td><td>Round Lab Birch Juice sun serum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brand says it suits</td><td>Normal, dry, dehydrated, combination</td><td>Oily, combination</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reapply</td><td>Every 2 to 3 hours</td><td>Every 2 hours</td></tr></table>

<h2>Why people love it, and why some do not</h2>
<p>I read through what shoppers say, and could not watch every video, so this is the overall picture from Google’s results and the brand’s page, not a quote from any one person.</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>The love.</strong> Comfortable, moisturizing, no white cast, a dewy finish that layers under makeup. The brand’s page shows 4.9 out of 5 from over 3,500 reviews, which is the brand’s own display.</li>
<li><strong>The “it depends.”</strong> Very oily skin can find the original too dewy, and some people say it stings the eyes.</li>
<li><strong>The doubts.</strong> Reddit threads on r/SkincareAddiction and r/SkinbarrierLovers include negative experiences, so I would not call it perfect for everyone.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How I would use it</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>Last step of the morning routine,</strong> after moisturizer.</li>
<li><strong>Two full fingers</strong> for face and neck, as the brand says. Don’t skimp, because the SPF number assumes a generous layer.</li>
<li><strong>Reapply every 2 to 3 hours</strong> when you are outside.</li>
<li><strong>Dry skin:</strong> layer it over a moisturizer. <strong>Oily skin:</strong> skip extra moisturizer, or choose Aqua-Fresh.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you want to compare it with other Korean sunscreens, see my <a href="/beauty/round-lab-birch-juice-sunscreen-review/">Round Lab Birch Juice review</a> and the <a href="/beauty/round-lab-camellia-deep-collagen-sun-serum-review/">Round Lab Camellia sun serum review</a>.</p>

<h2>My verdict</h2>
<p><strong>I use and prefer Aqua Rice</strong> for its lighter, milk-like texture. <strong>I’d pick the original</strong> if you want an affordable, creamy daily sunscreen with no white cast and a dewy finish. <strong>I’d pick Aqua-Fresh</strong> if your skin is oily or combination and the original feels too rich. <strong>I’d look elsewhere</strong> if chemical filters sting your eyes or you can only use mineral sunscreen, and I’d patch-test if you react to plant or ferment extracts.</p>

<blockquote class="quote">The original is the rich one, Aqua Rice is the light one, and Aqua Rice is my pick.</blockquote>

<div class="callout"><div class="up">A note</div><p>I’m not a doctor. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding, or have a skin condition, ask your doctor or dermatologist before adding a new product. See a doctor for any spot that is new, changing or bleeding.</p></div>

